A1. What you do on the musical comedy stage. ~ CONCENTRATE
A10. This lad can handle a liner (abbr.) ~ SS
A12. The kind of pedestrian that gets by. ~ ALERT
A13. Pertaining to the air. ~ AERO
A14. A busy buzzer. ~ BEE
A15. To bank on. ~ RELY
A16. Summer ermine. ~ MINK
A17. This is upstanding yet shady. ~ TREE
A18. This is what a friend is to Eddie Guest. ~ PAL
A19. These kind of men never part. ~ BALD
A20. One thousand iron men. ~ GRAND
A21. Printer's measure. ~ EN
A22. Ado. ~ FUSS
A23. You'll find this at a fence. ~ LOOT
A24. This is a mass. ~ HULK
A25. This is a free-for-all in Scotland. ~ RIOT
A26. This is what they called you in the old days. ~ YE
A27. Something a wise man would never do for a wife. ~ SEEK
A28. You can't raise this in Scotland. ~ FUND
A29. Thing (Latin) ~ RES
A30. Chicken salad. ~ MEAL
A31. It's easy to get a drink out of this. ~ CASK
A32. Good place to count sheep (Plu.) ~ LEAS
A33. This spreads the dirt. ~ ANT
A34. Irish. ~ ERSE
A35. This used to be caught in a net. ~ HAIR
A36. You can hear this now that Miss Bow talks. ~ IT
A37. This is one for you. ~ UNIT
A38. This is how you feel after going on a tear. ~ TORN
A40. Pass this one by. ~ SNOB
A41. The head of the house. ~ COOK
A42. Exist. ~ BE
A43. Dusters. ~ MAIDS
A45. To send packing. ~ ROUT
A46. Lots of time. ~ EON
